LIMITATIONS = (
    "Several limitations qualify these conclusions. First, the data are literature compilations: hardness values were "
    "obtained with different loads and instruments, processing routes are coded only in six broad classes, and grain "
    "size, interstitial content and phase fractions are missing for most records [@borg2020]. The repeat scatter in Table 1 "
    "is only an approximate noise floor, because repeat groups mix genuine inter-laboratory replicates with variation hidden "
    "inside a processing class and, for yield strength, with re-tabulated values (Table S15). The external hardness set, "
    "measured at a single load in a single laboratory, may also carry a systematic offset relative to the literature, which "
    "would inflate its RMSE but not its rank metrics. Second, no distribution-free guarantee covers the shifted protocols: "
    "the CV+ bound requires exchangeability, which P2–P6 violate by design, and the group-weighted variant is only "
    "asymptotically valid under hierarchical exchangeability. Deployment-matched calibration is a heuristic that targets the "
    "anticipated kind of novelty; the coverages reported here are empirical, marginal over the test alloys of each protocol, "
    "and specific to these data. They do not hold for every alloy system (the per-system coverage in Table S12 varies "
    "widely), and they would not transfer to a shift of a different kind, such as a new laboratory or processing route. "
    "For P6 in particular, the choice between element- and system-grouped calibration changed the coverage markedly; we "
    "fixed the element-grouped scheme beforehand from label-free information, but with a single external set this choice "
    "cannot be validated. The deployment-specific inner folds are also few (three for tuning, five for calibration), so the "
    "selected hyper-parameters are noisy (Table S16). Third, the cluster bootstrap treats alloy systems as exchangeable "
    "units; systems that share elements are not fully independent, so the intervals are approximate and probably somewhat "
    "narrow. TabPFN intervals were evaluated in the GPU environment and are summarized without bootstrap intervals, and the "
    "protocols emulate discovery "
    "retrospectively without the selection bias of a real campaign, in which candidates are chosen because a model predicts "
    "them to be extreme. Fourth, the benchmark covers two properties and composition-derived descriptors; structure-aware "
    "or microstructure-aware representations, physics-based strengthening models [@toda2015,varvenne2016,maresca2020] and "
    "newer foundation models were not evaluated. Finally, no new alloys were synthesized; the temporal and external tests "
    "are the closest available proxies for prospective validation, and closed-loop experimental validation remains the "
    "definitive test."
)

WHATS_NEXT = (
    "Machine-learning models for high-entropy alloys are usually judged by how well they reproduce data that closely "
    "resemble their training data. This study shows that the same models are considerably less accurate when asked the "
    "question that matters for discovery—what will an untried alloy do?—and that the coverage of their uncertainty "
    "estimates can be substantially improved, though not guaranteed, by calibrating them under the same kind of novelty. The next step is to make such extrapolation-aware "
    "evaluation the default: open benchmarks with frozen grouped, temporal and external splits, reporting of "
    "calibrated intervals and applicability-domain scores alongside every proposed alloy, and closed-loop campaigns in "
    "which the models' interval forecasts are tested by synthesis. Richer descriptions of processing and microstructure, "
    "physics-informed strengthening models and pre-trained materials foundation models are promising routes to narrowing "
    "the gap between interpolation and extrapolation that the present benchmark exposes."
)
